# Orchestra routing policy

GPT-5.6 Sol is Root / Tech Lead. Sol divides substantial tasks before
delegation, chooses role/model bindings and write scopes, decides whether work
can run in parallel, integrates all handoffs and performs final review.

Roles are logical bindings, not permanently fixed models. Initial bindings are
Root = GPT-5.6 Sol, Engineer = Grok 4.6, Frontend general = Qwen 3.8 Max via
Alibaba/Qwen Token Plan, and Visual/UI specialist = Kimi K3 via OpenCode Go.

Use Qwen for normal React, TypeScript, forms, state, API wiring, components,
responsive CSS and ordinary frontend implementation. Keep Qwen tasks bounded to
the assigned frontend files and dependency edges; avoid full-repository
inventories and broad checks unless Sol explicitly requests them. Use Grok for backend,
architecture implementation, debugging, tests, integration and general
engineering. Use Kimi selectively for visual redesign, screenshot-driven work,
complex UI composition, UX review, design-system refinement and visual polish.
Kimi does not delegate.

Cross-role delegation always passes through Sol. Qwen, Grok and Kimi never
call another primary worker. If a worker needs another specialty, it reports
the exact missing input to Sol; Sol asks the right specialist and returns the
relevant handoff. Do not form arbitrary chains such as Qwen → Grok → Kimi.

For full-stack work:

1. Inspect the repository and define shared contracts first.
2. Give every worker an explicit objective, allowed files and handoff format.
3. Delegate in parallel only when write scopes are disjoint.
4. Never let two workers write the same or shared file at once.
5. Otherwise delegate sequentially through Sol.
6. Root owns shared files, final review, integration and final verification.

Do not delegate trivial changes merely to use an agent. Retry a failed worker
at most once when failure is transient. Escalate unresolved ambiguity to root.
Never expose provider credentials to a subagent prompt.
